{
  "case_id": "C01",
  "audience": "General public with secondary-school science literacy",
  "target_duration_s": 60,
  "beats": [
    {
      "beat": "Hook",
      "narration": "You have probably heard that not getting enough sleep is bad for your heart. But researchers asked a different question: does it also matter whether you sleep at roughly the same time every night?",
      "on_screen": "Title card: 'How much you sleep - or when?' Two clocks, one steady, one jumping around.",
      "claim_refs": ["SYN-C01: Background"]
    },
    {
      "beat": "What they did",
      "narration": "Just over forty-one thousand adults, aged forty to sixty-nine, wore a wrist monitor for one week. Each person got a sleep regularity score from zero to one hundred. Researchers then watched their health records for about six years.",
      "on_screen": "41,208 adults, aged 40-69 - 7 days of wrist accelerometry - Sleep Regularity Index 0-100 - median 6.4 years follow-up.",
      "claim_refs": ["SYN-C01: Abstract", "SYN-C01: Methods"]
    },
    {
      "beat": "What they found",
      "narration": "In that time, one thousand eight hundred and seventy-three people had a first heart or metabolic illness. The fifth of people with the most irregular sleep timing had about a thirty-four percent higher rate of these events than the fifth with the steadiest timing.",
      "on_screen": "1,873 first events. Least regular fifth vs most regular fifth: 34% higher rate (hazard ratio 1.34, 95% CI 1.18-1.52).",
      "claim_refs": ["SYN-C01: Abstract", "SYN-C01: Results"]
    },
    {
      "beat": "A step-by-step pattern",
      "narration": "And the pattern was graded: as regularity fell group by group, the rate climbed step by step. That held after allowing for age, sex, weight, smoking, deprivation, shift work - and total hours slept.",
      "on_screen": "Bar chart of five quintiles: 1.00, 1.06, 1.13, 1.22, 1.34. Caption: adjusted for age, sex, BMI, smoking, deprivation, shift work, sleep duration.",
      "claim_refs": ["SYN-C01: Results", "SYN-C01: Abstract"]
    },
    {
      "beat": "The catch",
      "narration": "Important caveat: this study observed people, it did not test anything. Sleep was measured only once, over seven days, so it cannot show that irregular timing causes disease - something else may explain the link. Trials would be needed to find out. But a steadier bedtime costs nothing to try.",
      "on_screen": "Association, not proof. Measured once over 7 days; timing never re-measured. Next step: trials of sleep-timing stabilisation.",
      "claim_refs": ["SYN-C01: Study design", "SYN-C01: Limitations", "SYN-C01: Discussion"]
    }
  ]
}
